ls its nearest point to the origin if and only if\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{h_A=h_B=h_C\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad r\\\\le\\\\min_i h_i.}\n\\\\]\n\nTo prove the lemma, its centroid (c) is in the relative interior of (K). If it minimizes squared distance to the origin, derivatives in every direction tangent to \\\\(\\\\sum_i y_i=r\\\\) vanish. Hence\n\\\\[\nc_A=c_B=c_C=r/3.\n\\\\]\nConversely, if the centroid has these coordinates, it is the nearest point to the origin in the entire plane, so it is also nearest in (K).\n\nIt remains to characterize when the centroid coordinates are equal. Suppose (h_i<h_j). On the part of (K) with (y_j\\\\le h_i), swapping coordinates (i,j) preserves the region and area, so its integral of (y_j-y_i) is zero. On the remaining part, (y_j>h_i\\\\ge y_i), so that integral is strictly positive whenever this part has positive area. It has positive area exactly when (r>h_i): if (r\\\\le h_i), it is empty; if (r>h_i), choose\n\\\\[\n\\\\max\\\\{h_i,r-h_i-h_k\\\\}<y_j<\\\\min\\\\{h_j,r\\\\},\n\\\\]\nwhere (k) is the third index, and then choose (0<y_i<h_i), (0<y_k<h_k) with sum (r-y_j). The interval is nonempty because (r<\\\\sum h_i). These strict inequalities give a relatively open set of positive area. Consequently,\n\\\\[\nh_i<h_j\\\\ \\\\text{and}\\\\ r>h_i\\\\quad\\\\Longrightarrow\\\\quad c_i<c_j.\n\\\\]\nThus, if the caps are unequal and their minimum is less than (r), the centroid cannot have equal coordinates. In the other cases it does: equal caps give permutation symmetry; if (r\\\\le\\\\min h_i), all caps are redundant and (K) is the symmetric triangle \\\\(y_i\\\\ge0,\\\\sum y_i=r\\\\). This proves the lemma.\n\n**Translate the lemma into values.** Order the values as (a\\\\le b\\\\le c). The condition (r\\\\le\\\\min h_i) is equivalent to\n\\\\[\n\\\\min(b,d)+\\\\min(c,d)\\\\le d.\n\\\\]\nSince (b>0), this forces (c<d), and the inequality becomes (b+c\\\\le d=V-v_P), or (v_P\\\\le a). Conversely, (v_P\\\\le a) makes that inequality hold.\n\nFor the equal-cap condition, if (d\\\\le a), all caps equal (d); this is exactly (v_P\\\\ge b+c=V-a). If (d>a), equality of all caps forces (a=b=c), and equal winner values clearly suffice. This proves the stated condition.\n\n**Zero values.** If just two winner values (a,b) are positive, the zero-valued winner pays zero. The face is the segment\n\\\\[\np_1+p_2=v_P,\\\\qquad\n\\\\max(0,v_P-b)\\\\le p_1\\\\le\\\\min(a,v_P).\n\\\\]\nIts endpoints are\n\\\\[\n(\\\\ell_1,v_P-\\\\ell_1),\\\\qquad(v_P-\\\\ell_2,\\\\ell_2),\n\\\\]\nwhere \\\\(\\\\ell_1=(v_P-b)_+\\\\), \\\\(\\\\ell_2=(v_P-a)_+\\\\). Relative to the VCG vector, these endpoints are \\\\((0,r)\\\\) and \\\\((r,0)\\\\), so the nearest point is their midpoint, also the segment's centroid. With at most one positive winner value, (F) is a singleton.\n\n## Worked example where the points differ\n\nTake\n\\\\[\n(v_A,v_B,v_C)=(1,2,3),\\\\qquad v_P=2.\n\\\\]\nThen (V=6>2), VCG is \\\\((0,0,0)\\\\), and\n\\\\[\nF=\\\\{p\\\\ge0:p_A+p_B+p_C=2,\\\\ p_A\\\\le1\\\\}.\n\\\\]\n(The other two caps are redundant.) The nearest point to zero in its plane is feasible, so\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{p\"'^{'\"\\\\rm VN}=(2/3,2/3,2/3).}\n\\\\]\n\nFor the centroid, start with the full triangle having vertices \\\\((2,0,0),(0,2,0),(0,0,2)\\\\). Its centroid is \\\\((2/3,2/3,2/3)\\\\). The removed triangle has vertices \\\\((2,0,0),(1,1,0),(1,0,1)\\\\), centroid \\\\((4/3,1/3,1/3)\\\\), and one quarter of the original area. Therefore\n\\\\[\n\\\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n=\\\\frac{(2/3,2/3,2/3)-\\\\tfrac14(4/3,1/3,1/3)}{1-\\\\tfrac14}\n=\\\\boxed{(4/9,7/9,7/9)}.\n\\\\]\nThese points differ, as the condition predicts: \\\\(1<2<5\\\\), and the winner values are unequal.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '[sol] 2026-10-22: Drafted answer.md with full swap-integral proof, simplified exact condition, zero-value cases, and differing example. Please review shared file before final agreement. No need to duplicate drafting; independent verification most useful.' >> /work/board.md\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '[sol] 2026-10-22: Drafted answer.md with full swap-integral proof, simplified exact condition, zero-value cases, and differing example. Please review shared file before final agreement. No need to duplicate drafting; independent verification most useful.' >> /work/board.md\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"[sol] 2026-10-22: Read problem; deriving an exact centroid/projection equality condition via capped simplex geometry. Will independently characterize all parameter regions and coordinate findings.\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Key reduction: d=sum(v)-vP, l_i=(v_i-d)+, h_i=min(v_i,d), y=p-l. F becomes 0<=y_i<=h_i, sum y=r=sum(h)-d. Nearest is projection of 0. For positive 3D caps, equality requires centroid coordinates equal; swap symmetry shows E[y_i]<E[y_j] iff h_i<h_j and h_i<r. Thus equality iff all h_i equal OR r<=min h_i. For strictly positive winner values this simplifies to vP<=min(v_i), or vP>=sum(v_i)-min(v_i), or all winner values equal. Checking degeneracies next.\n[argon] 2026-10-22: Read problem and sol's reduction. I am verifying the reduction y = p - p^VCG, checking the minimum-revenue face R = max(v_P, sum l_i), and checking all boundary/degenerate cases (e.g. R = sum l_i when v_P <= sum l_i, or v_i = 0, etc.) via Python and analytical proof.\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Proposed final condition (nonnegative values): equality iff at least one winner value is zero, OR vP<=min(v_i), OR vP>=sum(v_i)-min(v_i), OR vA=vB=vC. With all winners positive, centroid interior forces y coordinates equal; swap argument gives classification above. Opus/argon: please verify independently and flag concerns; I will draft shared answer.md with this theorem and example (vA,vB,vC;vP)=(1,2,3;2), centroid=(4/9,7/9,7/9), nearest=(2/3,2/3,2/3).\n[opus] 20:37 Working on it. Key reduction: R=v_P, F={sum p=v_P, VCG_i<=p_i<=v_i}; in x=p-VCG coords F = simplex{x>=0,sum=D} cut by caps x_i<=u_i=min(v_i,s), s=sum v - v_P. Claim: equal iff min(u_i,D) equal for all i (reflection argument). Verifying numerically, will draft answer.md.\n[opus] 20:38 Independently verified sol's condition by Monte Carlo (20k random instances, exact polygon centroid vs water-filling projection): matches except 2 near-boundary tolerance artifacts (deviation is O(eps^2)). Example (1,2,3;2) confirmed: gap 2/9 in coord A. My proof of strict ordering: swap map sigma_ij sends F∩{x_i>x_j} into F∩{x_i<x_j} when h_i<h_j; leftover {x_j>h_i} has positive area iff h_i<r. Agree sol drafts answer.md.\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Drafted answer.md with full swap-integral proof, simplified exact condition, zero-value cases, and differing example. Please review shared file before final agreement. No need to duplicate drafting; independent verification most useful.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom fractions import Fraction as Q\nfrom itertools import product,combinations\nfrom math import atan2\n\ndef centroid(v,P):\n    a,b,c=map(Q,v); P=Q(P)\n    lines=[(1,0,0),(1,0,a),(0,1,0),(0,1,b),(1,1,P),(1,1,P-c)]\n    vertices=set()\n    for (u,w,k),(x,z,t) in combinations(lines,2):\n        det=u*z-w*x\n        if not det:continue\n        A=(k*z-w*t)/det;B=(u*t-k*x)/det;C=P-A-B\n        if 0<=A<=a and 0<=B<=b and 0<=C<=c:vertices.add((A,B))\n    mid=tuple(sum(pt[i] for pt in vertices)/len(vertices) for i in range(2))\n    pts=sorted(vertices,key=lambda p:atan2(float(p[1]-mid[1]),float(p[0]-mid[0])))\n    area=Q(0);mom=[Q(0),Q(0)]\n    for (x,y),(u,w) in zip(pts,pts[1:]+pts[:1]):\n        cross=x*w-u*y;area+=cross;mom[0]+=(x+u)*cross;mom[1]+=(y+w)*cross\n    A,B=(m/(3*area) for m in mom)\n    return (A,B,P-A-B)\ndef nearest(v,P):\n    d=sum(v)-P;l=[max(Q(0),x-d) for x in v];h=[x-z for x,z in zip(v,l)];r=P-sum(l)\n    for mask in range(7):\n        sat=[i for i in range(3) if mask>>i&1];free=[i for i in range(3) if not(mask>>i&1)]\n        t=(r-sum(h[i] for i in sat))/len(free)\n        if t>=0 and all(h[i]<=t for i in sat) and all(h[i]>=t for i in free):\n            return tuple(l[i]+min(h[i],t) for i in range(3))\nn=0\nfor vals in product(range(1,6),repeat=3):\n    v=tuple(map(Q,vals))\n    for m in range(1,2*sum(vals)):\n        P=Q(m,2)\n        predicted=P<=min(v) or P>=sum(v)-min(v) or len(set(v))==1\n        actual=centroid(v,P)==nearest(v,P)\n        assert predicted==actual,(v,P,predicted,centroid(v,P),nearest(v,P))\n        n+=1\nprint('Exact rational verification passed:',n,'instances')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
